      "writtenOn": "2025-10-29T04:51:17Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "1. it\u0027s deliberate for cache/TLB related info. I didn\u0027t see how software may utilize that information, especially when the vcpu is not pinned. If you know a certain usage relying on accurate cache/TLB info, then we should discuss how to achieve that (which is beyond cpuid) and any security concern letting the host manage. Otherwise I prefer to keeping it simple, as this patch does.\n\n2. FPU is managed by pKVM hence the state size must be set by pKVM (e.g. it doesn\u0027t sound secure if the host reports a smaller area size than supported FPU features to guest, leading to xsave/xrstor not covering the full state in guest). In this specific case the difference comes from:\n\n  a) crosvm gets size a88 from kvm-high, based on supported FPU features\n  b) crosvm passes a88 to kvm-high\n  c) kvm-high updates it to 240 based on initial xcr0 value. See kvm_set_cpuid() calls __kvm_update_cpuid_runtime()\n  d) kvm-high issues hypercall with 240 to pkvm, at the end of kvm_set_cpuid()\n  e) pkvm calls pkvm_enforce_cpuid(), which updates 240 to a88 based on supported FPU features\n  f) pkvm calls kvm_set_cpuid(), same as step c). Then 240 again\n\nat run-time, guest kernel updates xcr0, emulated by __kvm_set_xcr() which calls kvm_update_cpuid_runtime() to change 240 to a88 based on the new xcr0 value.\n  \nSo in the end the guest observes the desired value. btw there is an userspace utility called \u0027cpuid\u0027 to list all leafs/bits in readable format. You can use it to compare the end output before and after applying this series. 😊\n\n3. It\u0027s cleared later in kvm_update_pv_runtime():\n        struct kvm_cpuid_entry2 *best \u003d kvm_find_kvm_cpuid_features(vcpu);\n        \n        /*\n         * save the feature bitmap to avoid cpuid lookup for every PV\n         * operation\n         */\n        if (best) {\n                /* Disable PV features for pkvm protected vm */\n                if (pkvm_is_protected_vcpu(vcpu))\n                        best-\u003eeax \u003d 0; \n\nI try to keep the same style as other cpuid functions in pkvm_get_cpuid(), to collect all possible leaves first which are then sanitized in various latter places. But if you prefer to removing the info at the first place, I could do that too.\n\n4. the entire hybrid topology support is broken. we discussed earlier that it\u0027s unnecessary to expose virtual hybrid to the guest in pKVM. and similar to cache/TLB, such info is misleading w/o pinning the vcpu. that\u0027s why I didn\u0027t reuse the host bits here. allowing it is easy, but maybe it\u0027s better to not do it until seeing a real problem then we can better understand the usage to support?",
      "parentUuid": "718f9e72_bd5944b3",
      "revId": "d61fe9755076eea43145357b6cf2e8bd378e2a73",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
